prs.migration:
Replaces zella_audit_privileged(). The guard requires zella_function_archive. It compares md5(pg_get_functiondef) with the production md5, read from zprod3 and confirmed with a read only production fingerprint after approval. If the migrated md5 is already in place it exits; otherwise it stops with VERSION_MISMATCH. It archives the old body first.

New body:
- the actor is coalesce(auth.uid(), the x-zella-actor value from current_setting('request.headers', true));
- the header is trusted only when the JWT role is service_role and the value is a uuid of an active platform admin;
- anything else gives a null actor, and parse errors never raise;
- rows are tagged new_data.via='admin_function'.

The triggers are not recreated. The rollback checks the migrated md5 and restores the archived body exactly. It touches neither zella_set_business_size nor zella_business_readiness.

prs.tests:
Isolated database security suite (DB-14):
- the migration runs twice (idempotent);
- the rollback runner goes forward, back and forward;
- service_role claims plus the header set the actor;
- the same header from authenticated or anon is ignored;
- a uuid that isn't an admin, or an inactive admin, is ignored.
Deno: the header comes only from the verified admin id, never from the body. Browser harness: one suspend shows one readable grouped entry.

prs.migration:
For zella_admin_automation_set_status and each zella_admin_ops function that computes business_live (sql/zella_admin_ops.sql:628, 662, 689):
- check to_regprocedure;
- archive with zella_function_archive;
- compare md5 with production and with the migrated value (exit if already applied, otherwise VERSION_MISMATCH).
The rollback restores the archived bodies exactly. Confirm that none of these functions are in zella_admin_permissions.sql's md5 list.

Creates zella_admin_request_activation(p_business_id, p_reason) with zella_admin_require on the permission the owner chooses. It:
- refuses suspended or cancelled businesses;
- refuses when zella_activation_report fails (it calls the report, not a copy);
- applies the same updates as zella_request_activation;
- audits old and new onboarding_status and is_active with the reason.
The guard stops if the signature exists with an unexpected md5, and checks the md5 of zella_request_activation and zella_activation_report against the versions it mirrors (VERSION_MISMATCH on drift). The rollback drops the function and revokes execute. It replaces neither the report nor readiness.

prs.deploy_order:
No code. It records results the owner approved:
